  {
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": ">  Could you elaborate further on when it may not work? If I pass a numpy array in, the original code has the pointer/ref count, I'm not sure I'm following your explanation of when/where the risk occurs? Presumably that would be fine with multiple threads via the prange function\r\n\r\nYou know. I'm thinking back on this and I think this atomic code should be safe. I was confusing this hack with other hacks I did around the same time. I think the main problem with this code is probably mostly that I suspect it can generate invalid LLVM code if some dtypes are passed in, since I don't check which LLVM instructions really exist (the atomic instructions only exist at specific types). I also never created the CAS loop fallback.\r\n\r\n> Also, which dtypes does it support?\r\n\r\nIntegers up to 64 bits (probably? it might just be 32 and 64-bits), and floats of 32 and 64 bits. I think. Those are definitely the only ones I've tested. Signed ints work fine since in LLVM they are not different types, they are different instructions.\r\n",
